  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nThrough next week (Friday)...\n\nThis weekend: Sprawling mid level high at the surface and aloft \n(595 to 597DM) remains situated over the ArkLaTex, per RAP/upper\nair analysis and GOES East water vapor imagery. Continued\nanomalous some 8-15F above normal (95F to 101F). Drier air is \npropagating westward, with GOES East total PWs showing drier air\nin the west (1.2 to 1.4 inches) while a little deeper moisture to\nthe east. A westward propagating upper low/surface trough over \nthe northeast Gulf will lead to an influx of deeper moisture aloft\nfrom east to west and higher boundary layer moisture, which \nsupports less mixing and increased concerns of dangerous heat.\nHighs will continue to moderate into the upper 90s to around 100F\n(97-102F), with 101-102F possible, especially in northern \nobservation sites. Continued messaging targeted heat graphics in \nthe HWO through the weekend (Elevated in west-central to\nnortheast, east and southeast MS while and Elevated west of \nInterstate 55 late week Sunday into Monday). Ongoing heat advisory\nlooks on track but some localized expansion may be warranted\nfurther south and west today. Additional heat headlines will\nlikely be needed Sunday but will hold off introduction for now. Lows\nwill be seasonably warm, some 6-14F above and a touch warmer this\nmorning (69-74F) with even more seasonable warmth tonight, 8-15F \nabove (72-76F). \n\nRain chances remain a low chance (less than 15 percent), with any\nchances possible southeast of the Natchez Trace into the Highway \n45 to Interstate 59 corridors. While the official forecast indicate\na low chance of rain (less than 15 percent), some consensus and \nsynoptic pattern could support higher coverage than indicated, as \nmuch as isolated/low chance rain and storm coverage in the Golden \nTriangle today and additionally southeast of the Natchez Trace \ninto the the Pine Belt late weekend on Sunday (15-30 percent).\n\nNext week (Through next Friday): As the upper low weakens and \nanother shortwave/cold core low propagates into the Mid West \nStates into the middle of next week, the mid level feature over \nthe northeast Gulf is progged to lift northward. This feature will\nbecome absorbed in the mean flow to the north, which may become \nmore amplified than previously anticipated but has trended \nstronger with the ArkLaTex to northwest Gulf high pressure ridge \naloft. This will lead to less focused moisture convergence but \npersistent deep moisture (PWs around 1.75 to greater than 2 \ninches).There are continued indications of weak front/convergence \nzone and northwesterly flow sinking into the area early next week,\nwith drier air and lower dewpoints late week. \n\nDangerous heat concerns will persist into early week and will \ncontinue to message Elevated HWO heat graphics for Monday, less \noppressive/suppressed heat/Elevated HWO Tuesday and much more \npleasant into late week. Combined with the ridge less stout and \ncentered over east TX and weak front diving southward, highs will\nnot be as seasonably warm later (10F to 16F above early week to \n3F to 10F above late week). Highs peak in the 96F to 101F range \nearly week to 90F to 96F by mid week to 86F to 94F by the end of\nthe week. Rain and storm chances remain in the isolated/low \nchance range (15-25 percent) on average in north central,\nnortheast to east and southeast MS, with deep moisture/frontal \nconvergence possibly driving coverage a touch higher in the \nscattered/medium chance range (30 to 50 percent range). There \ncould be some cooler thermal profiles aloft (around -8C at 500mb\nand vertical totals in the mid to upper 20s (27C to 29C); \nisolated strong storms cannot be ruled out at times especially\nearly week around Monday. /DC/",
